[{:db/id "q-99656/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "adad8401-def9-5d83-9a33-625586cce514" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99656/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "7d5bb78f-7e7a-5366-9bf5-e2b2f7d9c4f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99656/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "fbe1d794-2156-5187-84e2-a72778546d2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99656/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "52be6e67-1227-5575-839d-7ae9a7e5f2cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99656/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e7c564a6-d53f-5fba-ad68-5b256e4beb6a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99656/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "48d69382-2c32-541e-9268-6a09029af2a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99656/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-99656/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-99656/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-99656/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-99656/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-99656/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99656" :question/id #uuid "060ce9a2-3540-5cf5-bd0c-22d5fb5ca811" :question/math-academy-id "q-99656" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(4)+2(1)(-1)+2(-1)(1) \\\\  & =4-2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(4)(0)+2(-1)(1)+2(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-2+2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(0)+2(1)(1)+2(-1)(1) \\\\  & =0+2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-99656/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99656"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99875/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "16e588d1-8adb-59d7-9046-f6dd633f23a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99875/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "aaec5265-6573-5933-9b8d-ce478389fb9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99875/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "323c48b0-a01f-52cb-980c-e84debe1b0d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99875/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "7d999d26-f7f9-5f96-92c8-3f0034a3cc92" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99875/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "69bccc3e-f0a0-5476-9f25-0c6d50dadc9e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99875/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e72d34d8-40fd-5549-b70f-2560bcf6d144" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99875/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-99875/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-99875/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-99875/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-99875/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-99875/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99875" :question/id #uuid "d960b676-bf56-5e24-b2c3-a69d5f033466" :question/math-academy-id "q-99875" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(1)+3(-1)(3)+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =1-9+8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(4)+3(3)(0)+2(2)(-1) \\\\  & =4+0-4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(4)+3(-1)(0)+2(2)(-1) \\\\  & =4+0-4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-99875/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99875"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-98523"] :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(6)(-1)+2(0)(1)+3(-2)(-1) \\\\  & =-6+0+6 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(6)(1)+2(0)(1)+3(-2)(0) \\\\  & =6+0+0 \\\\  & =6\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{3},$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-99878/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "5ebf5199-79d7-573e-8447-60712d518818" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99878/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "df127452-27f1-574c-962d-b1fd57e1f262" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99878/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "1131fc11-e294-5f87-8a6a-016bfd85a143" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99878/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "d33d6920-cb2c-5bd6-bb68-734bc2831398" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99878/selection/bf26c77e3689065e6bb4fa6e6284884695a5845d5d033e0cc134697b517bb193" :answer/id #uuid "a93b7f9d-db47-5e55-aabb-7d4d1fa95f00" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99878/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "71a09e45-f26a-5a62-ac12-061709bcbfc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99878/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-99878/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-99878/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-99878/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-99878/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-99878/selection/bf26c77e3689065e6bb4fa6e6284884695a5845d5d033e0cc134697b517bb193"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99878" :question/id #uuid "fbffa268-7521-5240-b69f-7fff639df47e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99878" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{1}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =2(1)(5)+(1)(-2)+(2)(-4) \\\\  & =10-2-8 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(1)(0)+(1)(2)+(2)(-1) \\\\  & =0+2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =2(5)(0)+(-2)(2)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =0-4+4 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-99878/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99878"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99635/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee01